<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thirty Comrades</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1941">1941</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Thirty Comrades were young Burmese nationalists, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aung-san">Aung San</a>, who received secret military training from the Japanese on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hainan">Hainan</a> in 1941. In December 1941 they founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma-independence-army">Burma Independence Army</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a> and entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> with the Japanese invasion of 1942. Several became leading figures of independent Burma, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ne-win">Ne Win</a>, who seized power in 1962.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thirty-comrades.jpg" alt="Thirty Comrades" width="320" height="185" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burma_Independence_Army.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In April 1941 small groups of young Burmese left the country in secret to obtain military training for a struggle against British colonial rule. They were sent by the Dobama Asiayone, the We Burmans Association, under the leadership of Thakin Aung San, and had meant to seek help in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a>. Instead they met the Japanese at Amoy, went on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, and were flown to occupied Sanya to be trained by the Japanese Army. For security they were later moved to Formosa, and they returned towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> by way of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>.</p><p>Their mentor and chief trainer was the Japanese officer Suzuki Keiji, known to the Burmese as Bo Mogyo, Commander Thunderbolt, who headed the Minami Kikan, a special intelligence unit formed to support a national uprising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>. Each of the comrades took a nom de guerre.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>On 26 December 1941, in a house in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a> owned by a Burmese doctor, about 25 of the Thirty Comrades drew blood from their arms into a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> bowl and each drank from it, the thway thauk of Burmese military tradition, pledging eternal loyalty to one another and to Burmese independence. Their average age was 24. The group formed the core of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma-independence-army">Burma Independence Army</a>, the beginning of the modern Burmese army, and the British were driven out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>.</p><p>The historian Gordon H. Luce, who taught at Rangoon University before the war, held that the Thirty Comrades under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aung-san">Aung San</a> helped found the fourth Burmese state in history, after those of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anawrahta">Anawrahta</a>, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabinshwehti">Tabinshwehti</a> and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaungpaya">Alaungpaya</a>. Ba Maw, head of state from August 1943 under the administration established by the Japanese, took a somewhat different view of their role. Bo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ne-win">Ne Win</a> ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> from March 1962 and died on 5 December 2002. The last of the comrades, Bo Ye Htut, died on 28 November 2013 at the age of 92.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thirty Comrades” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Japan invaded British Burma in 1942 with the help of the Burma Independence Army, formed by Aung San and the <b>Thirty Comrades</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-burma">State of Burma</a></li></ul></section>
